SimTower releases ClipCursor and immediately destroys its floor-selection popup.
The asynchronous WM_WINE_CLIPCURSOR release still targets that popup, so Wine
drops it when retrieving hardware messages and leaves the X11 clipping window
grabbed. Subsequent clicks are constrained to the vanished popup's rectangle.

If the release target has disappeared, deliver the release to the surviving
active window in the same input queue. Keep ordinary input and live targets
unchanged, and do not redirect requests that enable clipping.

diff --git a/server/queue.c b/server/queue.c
--- a/server/queue.c
+++ b/server/queue.c
@@ -1845,6 +1845,13 @@ static user_handle_t find_hardware_message_window( struct desktop *desktop, struct
 
     if (!*thread)
         *thread = get_window_thread( win );
+    if (!*thread && input && msg->msg == WM_WINE_CLIPCURSOR && (msg->wparam & SET_CURSOR_NOCLIP))
+    {
+        /* A popup may be destroyed before its queued cursor release is handled. */
+        win = input_shm->active;
+        *thread = get_window_thread( win );
+    }
+
     return win;
 }
 
